# RME invoice — branch scope from the EXPLICIT actor (rule 198)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/fix-rme-invoice-branch-scope-explicit-actor-1.md`.
Extends rule 197 (§E payment explicit actor). **Supersedes rule 197 §30 for
`RmeInvoiceService`** — that residual is CLOSED. The other half of §30 (the shared
`RmeWorkingBranchScope::branchIdsFor(null)` null contract) is NOT changed here and
stays recorded (§30 below). No owner decision is involved: this is a technical
security fix inside existing authority; the decision register is unchanged.

## A. Root cause (verified, reproduced on the unmodified base)
1. `RmeInvoiceService::cashierScopeBranchIds()` returned
   `RmeWorkingBranchScope::branchIdsFor(Auth::user())` for the cashier pending list
   (`paginatePendingVisits`) and the doctor→cashier handoff queue
   (`cashierHandoffQueue`). It judged whoever was logged in, not the actor the
   caller meant; and with NO session `branchIdsFor(null)` answers EVERY active RME
   branch, so both lists failed OPEN. Reproduced on `3d7ca206`: no session → visits
   of both fixture branches returned; ambient foreign cashier → the ambient branch.
2. Reachability (measured, not claimed): both methods are called ONLY by
   `RmeInvoiceController::index|handoff`, behind `auth` +
   `permission:manage_rme_billing` + `authorize('viewAny')`. Over HTTP the session
   user IS the actor, so it was a LATENT defect, not an exploited one. It became a
   real defect for any non-HTTP caller (command, job, future service).
3. Found in the same audit and fixed: `create()` took an explicit `$cashier` but
   performed NO service-level usability/permission check — an inactive or deleted
   account, an unsaved object carrying a governance id, or an active account
   without `manage_rme_billing` could bill a visit through a non-HTTP caller
   (reproduced red: "The invoice was created"). `findInBranch()` read
   `BranchContext` (the ambient session) and had ZERO callers — removed.

## B. The contract
4. Explicit actor requirement: every actor-scoped entry point declares a
   NON-NULLABLE, REQUIRED `User` parameter —
   `paginatePendingVisits(User $actor, …)`, `cashierHandoffQueue(User $actor, …)`,
   `findInvoiceForVisit(ClinicVisit, User $actor)`, `create(ClinicVisit, User $cashier, …)`.
   A null actor is a `TypeError` at the boundary; there is no overload that omits
   it and no default.
5. Trusted actor source: the controller's authenticated user
   (`$request->user()`), behind the existing `auth` middleware and policies. The
   service never accepts an actor id from the browser; a request-supplied
   `cashier_id` is ignored (validated data carries items/notes only).
6. Controller → service propagation: `RmeInvoiceController` passes
   `$request->user()` to all three read paths and to `create()`. Controller
   policies (`viewAny`, `create`) still run FIRST; the service check is
   independent and does not rely on them.
7. No ambient fallback: `RmeInvoiceService` code contains no `Auth::`, `auth()`,
   `app('auth')`, `request()`, `Request::`, `session()` or `BranchContext` (a
   comment-stripping governance test pins it, as rule 197 §23 does for the payment
   service). Never reintroduce one; never substitute the session user for a
   missing actor. (`AuditLogService::log` falls back to `auth()->user()` only for a
   NULL actor; the invoice service always passes the explicit one.)

## C. The single billing-actor authority
8. `RmeBillingActorGuard` (app/Modules/RmeInvoice/Services) is the ONE answer to
   "is this explicit actor a usable billing account?", shared by
   `RmeInvoiceService` and `RmePaymentService`. Both services resolve the
   persisted account ONCE and then ask the billing permission AND the working
   branch of that account — never of the object the caller passed in (security
   review F2: the payment half previously asked both of the passed object, so a
   stale in-memory role could have let invoice and payment disagree). Never add a
   second resolver.
9. `resolveUsable()` re-reads the account from the DATABASE: persisted, key
   present, `is_active = true`, not soft-deleted. An unsaved object carrying a real
   id, an unknown id, a deleted account, or a stale in-memory "active" copy of a
   since-deactivated account all resolve to null.
10. `resolveBillingActor()` additionally requires `manage_rme_billing`, evaluated
    on the PERSISTED account (stale in-memory roles cannot grant it). Super Admin
    passes through `Gate::before` exactly as in the policy — no new authority.
11. Branch scope is then asked of `RmeWorkingBranchScope` with the PERSISTED
    account (`branchIdsFor()` for the read lists, `allows()` for creation) — the
    canonical scope resolver; never re-derived, never hardcoded branches.

## D. Fail-closed behaviour
12. Read lists (pending visits, handoff queue): an unusable or unauthorized actor
    gets an EMPTY scope (`whereIn([])` ⇒ no rows) — never the estate, never MAIN,
    never the first branch, never a request `branch_id`.
13. Existing-invoice lookup: disclosed only when the invoice ROW's branch is in the
    actor's scope; otherwise null, indistinguishable from "no invoice".
14. Creation: inside the transaction, immediately after the visit row lock (a
    nonexistent visit is a 404 from that lookup first), an unusable or non-billing
    actor is refused with `AuthorizationException` (HTTP 403, never a 500) BEFORE
    the RME-branch check, the working-branch check, the status / finalized-RME /
    active-invoice checks, pricing, the invoice number and the audit row. Then the
    existing working-branch check (`allows`, rule 194 F1) runs on the persisted
    account. A refused attempt writes NOTHING (no invoice, no number, no audit).
14a. The billing form (`RmeInvoiceController::create`) returns `View|RedirectResponse`:
    when the visit already has an active invoice it redirects to that invoice. It
    was declared `: View`, so that ordinary path (a stale "Buat Tagihan" link
    after a colleague billed, back+reload, a direct URL) threw a `TypeError` = 500
    since Sprint 20 (security review F1, pre-existing; fixed here because the
    caller inventory reached it and rule "no 500 on an expected path").
15. Unchanged: a context-bound role (Kasir, Admin Klinik, Perawat; Front Office as
    their union) without a valid working context ⇒ empty scope; a request branch
    filter can only NARROW (`where branch_id` ANDed with `whereIn(scope)`).

## E. Roles — no new authority
16. Super Admin: governance scope (every active RME branch) as before; NOT a
    system actor, never used implicitly.
17. Kasir / Front Office: exactly what `RmeWorkingBranchScope` already answers for
    them (a context-bound account: its selected working branch), unchanged.
18. Admin Klinik: unchanged (its existing permission set decides).
19. A cross-branch exception exists only where already approved (governance
    roles); none is created here.
20. Background callers: there are NONE for `RmeInvoiceService` (no job, command,
    listener or schedule). A future one must pass a real, authorized human actor;
    there is no system cashier, and none may be invented. Absent one, it fails
    closed.

## F. Financial invariants preserved (verified by regression)
21. Manual pricing D21 (rule 194): unchanged — the cashier types the price, server
    computes integer cents, no tariff lookup, provenance MANUAL.
22. Issued invoices stay immutable; amounts change only through the correction
    workflow (rules 190/191). No new mutation route.
23. Payment explicit actor (rule 197 §E): unchanged behaviour; usability now asked
    of the shared guard.
24. D10 payment block under the invoice lock (rule 192) and invoice-version binding
    (rule 191): unchanged.
25. PostgreSQL transactions/locks: the actor check runs inside the existing
    per-visit `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` transaction; no new lock, no lock-order change.
26. Financial audit: the invoice + its audit row still commit together; a refused
    actor writes no audit row (consistent with the existing branch refusal).
27. Snapshots v0/vN, receivable totals, price provenance: untouched.
28. SPN4 pilot (rule 195), maker user 29 / checker user 1 (rule 197 D1e),
    D1/D1a/D1b/D1c/D1d: untouched — the correction service and its identity guard
    are not modified.
29. Finance Klinik / Data Analyst remain read-only; RoleSeeder unchanged.

25a. Cost: each guard resolution is ~3 queries (the `users` row plus the role and
    permission loads of a fresh instance); one per cashier list request, one
    inside the creation transaction. No N+1. Authorization is never cached across
    requests.

## G. Recorded residuals (NOT changed here — separate, wider changes)
30. `RmeWorkingBranchScope::branchIdsFor(null)` still answers every active RME
    branch. After this rule neither `RmeInvoiceService` nor `RmePaymentService` can
    pass null (non-nullable parameters). Controllers still hand the nullable
    `request()->user()` to the scope directly — `RmeInvoiceController::rmeBranches()`
    (handoff branch selector, receivables list and export) and `RmeReportController`
    — safe today only because every such route sits inside `auth`. The shared null
    contract is consumed across modules and is a separate change.
30a. No middleware enforces `is_active` per request (pre-existing). An account
    deactivated while its session is live now gets an EMPTY pending list and
    handoff queue and a 403 on billing, but policy-only surfaces (invoice show,
    receivables, export, the billing form GET) still answer from the session
    user's permissions until the session ends. Recorded, not changed here.
    **[CLOSED by rule 200 §E22 — HARDENING-RME-ACTIVE-USER-PATIENT-SELECTION-1:
    `EnsureAccountIsActive` stops a deactivated account's session on its next web
    request, before any controller; `RmeBillingActorGuard` now delegates the
    active-account read to the shared `ActiveAccountResolver`.]**
31. `ClinicVisitService` (`:94`, `:143`) still reads `Auth::user()` for the visit
    list scope — visit domain, out of scope; candidate
    `FIX-CLINIC-VISIT-BRANCH-SCOPE-EXPLICIT-ACTOR-1`.
    **CLOSED by AUDIT-RME-CLINIC-VISIT-BRANCH-AUTHORITY-1 — see rule 199.** The
    audit found 8 `Auth::user()`/`Auth::id()` sites and one `BranchContext` read
    (not 2), all now replaced by the explicit actor; it also closed two
    HTTP-reachable cross-branch reads (prior-visit picker, RME dashboard). §30 is
    unchanged.

